## Variable

Use `:=` and `=` to declare and define a variable, respectively. When using `:=`, the type of the variable is inferred from the value on the right side. Once a variable has been assigned a type, it cannot change. So, values assigned to that variable needs to be of the correct type.

The compiler checks that the type of variable is consistent with its use. In the following example, the variable `a` has type `int32` and cannot be used as a return value, which expect a `float32` type.

```
package main

func Main() float32 {
    a := int32(2)
    return a // ERROR: cannot use int32 as float32
}
```

```code:main
package main

func Main() int64 {
    a := 2
    a = 3
    return a
}
```
